// markdownlint configuration for the documents at the ROOT of `docs/`. markdownlint-cli2 reads the
// config nearest each linted file, so `docs/adr/` and `docs/runbooks/` keep their own — and
// `docs/architecture/` and `docs/design/` carry one each for the sole purpose of NOT inheriting this
// one. See the note at the bottom.
{
  // MD040 (fenced-code-language) is OFF here for the reason recorded in `docs/adr/.markdownlint.jsonc`
  // and repeated in `docs/runbooks/`: these documents fence MEASURED OUTPUT, and measured output has
  // no language.
  //
  // MEASURED, with the linter rather than with a grep, which matters because a grep for a bare fence
  // also counts the CLOSING fence of every tagged block and roughly doubles the answer. The honest
  // count: markdownlint reports exactly FOUR MD040 hits across the root of `docs/`, all four in
  // `engineering-traps.md`, and `docs/api/` has none. All four are transcripts — an HMAC round-trip
  // showing what `datetime2` does to a formatted timestamp, a `SqlServerRetryingExecutionStrategy`
  // exception copied verbatim, a table of which GUID forms each parser accepts, and three sort
  // orders for the same ten values. None of them is source and none of them has a language.
  //
  // WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The convention was settled twice in writing and this document already
  // followed it, but no config sat anywhere above it — so MD040 fired on all four, and a review on
  // PR #129 asked for `text` on the two that happened to be in the diff. Adding it to those two
  // would have made them the odd ones out among their own siblings and re-opened a question this
  // repository has now answered across #94, #96, #127 and #129, with the linter itself asking in
  // BOTH directions. That is the failure the ADR config named: "a rule a tool cannot read is a rule
  // that gets re-litigated." It was encoded in two directories, the third was left uncovered, and it
  // was duly re-litigated in the third.
  //
  // WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is deliberately NOT a repository-wide exemption. Outside `adr/` and
  // `runbooks/` the corpus runs the OTHER way, and the two sibling configs this change adds are what
  // keep the rule there. COUNTED RECURSIVELY, because that is the scope those configs actually
  // govern: 41 MD040 hits under `docs/architecture/**` and 293 under `docs/design/**` — 334, not the
  // 153 an earlier draft of this comment claimed. That number came from globbing each directory's
  // ROOT only, `docs/design/*.md` rather than `**/*.md`, which left 181 hits in 20 files
  // uncounted. Nobody has read any of them to establish whether they are measured output or untagged
  // source, so they keep the rule until somebody does. The integers drift as documents are added;
  // the direction is the durable part.
  "MD040": false
}
